Lab approach
Use three environments:
Environment	Use
Lambda Cloud single-GPU VM	nvidia-smi, CUDA containers, DCGM, GPU diagnostics, single-node NCCL, storage tests
Local Windows/WSL or VMs	Linux, Ansible, Slurm concepts, runbooks, architecture diagrams
NVIDIA course labs/simulators	BCM, UFM, CVT, BlueField, physical hardware workflows that cannot be authentically reproduced on a cloud VM


Do not claim hands-on BCM, UFM, CVT, or BlueField administration unless you complete a real NVIDIA course lab or have physical equipment access.
Weekly commitment
- 12–15 hours/week
- 7–9 hours labs
- 3–4 hours concepts/documentation
- Every week: publish one sanitized GitHub artifact in 04-runbooks-benchmarks-troubleshooting/
Month 1 — Bring-up, physical layer, and control plane
Week 1 — GPU server bring-up and acceptance
Exam focus: System and Server Bring-up
Learn:
- DGX vs HGX vs generic GPU server
- GPU, CPU, PCIe, NUMA, NVLink/NVSwitch, HBM
- BMC, OOB management, TPM, BIOS/UEFI
- Management, storage, and high-speed network planes
- Day 0 / Day 1 / Day 2 operations
Hands-on Lambda GPU lab:
nvidia-smi
nvidia-smi -q
nvidia-smi topo -m
nvidia-smi dmon -s pucvmet
lspci | grep -i nvidia
lscpu
numactl --hardware
lsblk
Create:
- GPU Server Day-0 Acceptance Checklist
- HGX 8-GPU architecture diagram
- Asset inventory template: serial number, GPU model, NIC, firmware, rack/U position, BMC IP, switch port
Deliverable: gpu-server-acceptance-checklist.md
Week 2 — Firmware, power, cooling, cabling, and fault isolation
Exam focus: firmware upgrades, physical GPUs, power/cooling, cables/transceivers
Learn:
- Firmware compatibility: BMC, BIOS, GPU, NIC, switch, NVSwitch, BlueField, transceiver
- PCIe device discovery and GPU enumeration
- Power budget, redundant power feeds, cooling/airflow
- DAC vs AOC vs optical transceivers
- Common failures: missing GPU, fan fault, ECC/Xid error, PCIe issue, bad cable, incorrect optic
Hands-on:
nvidia-smi -q -d ECC
nvidia-smi -q -d POWER
nvidia-smi -q -d TEMPERATURE
nvidia-smi -q -d PERFORMANCE
dmesg | grep -i -E "nvidia|xid|pcie"
lspci -vv
Create 10 incident scenarios, for example:
- GPU not visible after reboot
- GPU overheating under load
- PCIe link speed below expected
- Firmware mismatch after maintenance
- Link down after cable replacement
Deliverable: hardware-fault-troubleshooting-matrix.md
Week 3 — Control plane: BCM, OS provisioning, Slurm, Enroot, Pyxis
Exam focus: 19% Control Plane Installation and Configuration
Learn:
- BCM architecture: head node, images, categories, overlays, node states, HA
- Provisioning lifecycle: PXE → OS image → configuration → drivers → validation
- Slurm concepts: controller, compute nodes, partitions, accounts, QoS, jobs
- Enroot and Pyxis: containers for Slurm/HPC workloads
- NVIDIA driver, CUDA, Container Toolkit, NGC CLI
Hands-on:
- Install Slurm on local Ubuntu VMs or one controller/one worker setup.
- Submit a basic Slurm job.
- Build a GPU-ready node configuration checklist.
- On Lambda, run CUDA in a container:
docker run --rm --gpus all \
  nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04 nvidia-smi
- Install and validate NVIDIA Container Toolkit.
- Create an NGC account and install NGC CLI if available.
Deliverable: gpu-cluster-provisioning-runbook.md
Note: BCM itself is best practiced through NVIDIA’s AI Infrastructure Professional workshop/lab. The NCP-AII blueprint specifically tests BCM, Slurm, Enroot, and Pyxis. NCP-AII blueprint
Week 4 — BlueField, DOCA, MIG, and AI-factory topology
Exam focus: Physical Layer Management
Learn:
- BlueField DPU vs ConnectX NIC vs SuperNIC
- DPU mode vs NIC mode
- RShim, OOB management, representors, embedded switch
- DOCA purpose and driver/BF-Bundle lifecycle
- MIG: GPU partitioning, profiles, isolation, suitable workloads
- Rail-optimized AI-factory network design
Hands-on:
- Build a 64-GPU AI-factory topology:
  - 8 × 8-GPU servers
  - management network
  - storage network
  - InfiniBand or RoCE/Ethernet compute fabric
  - BlueField/ConnectX placement
- Use NVIDIA DOCA documentation or emulator/container where accessible.
- Create a MIG decision matrix: when to use full GPU, MIG, or fractional GPU scheduling.
Deliverable: bluefield-mig-ai-factory-design.md
Month 2 — Testing, verification, performance, and troubleshooting
Week 5 — GPU diagnostics, stress testing, and DCGM
Exam focus: Cluster Test and Verification
Learn:
- GPU health baselining
- DCGM diagnostics, policy, telemetry, and error reporting
- GPU utilization, memory, power, temperature, ECC, Xid errors
- Difference between a health test, burn-in test, and workload benchmark
Hands-on:
On Lambda, install/use DCGM where supported. Run:
nvidia-smi
nvidia-smi dmon -s pucvmet
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,temperature.gpu,power.draw,utilization.gpu,memory.used \
  --format=csv -l 5
If dcgmi is available:
dcgmi discovery -l
dcgmi diag -r 1
Deploy DCGM Exporter with Prometheus/Grafana if your GPU lab supports Kubernetes.
Deliverable: GPU health dashboard screenshots plus gpu-health-baseline-report.md.
Week 6 — NCCL, NVLink/NVSwitch, HPL, and performance validation
Exam focus: NCCL, HPL, NeMo, ClusterKit, burn-in
Learn:
- What NCCL validates
- Intra-node: PCIe vs NVLink vs NVSwitch
- Inter-node: InfiniBand/RoCE fabric bandwidth
- HPL purpose: compute-performance validation
- ClusterKit purpose: multi-dimensional cluster assessment
- NeMo burn-in purpose: realistic AI-workload validation
Hands-on:
nvidia-smi topo -m
nvidia-smi nvlink -s
Run a single-node NCCL test if your Lambda image/container supports it. Capture:
- GPU count
- Topology
- Collective tested
- Message sizes
- Bandwidth
- Errors or anomalies
Do not invent multi-node results. Instead, write the multi-node validation procedure, success thresholds, expected evidence, and remediation path.
Deliverable: nccl-hpl-cluster-validation-plan.md
Week 7 — Fabric, cable, transceiver, and storage validation
Exam focus: CVT concepts, signal quality, UFM, storage testing
Learn:
- CVT: what it validates and where it fits in deployment
- UFM: topology, Subnet Manager, health, link state, bandwidth, congestion
- ibstat, ibdiagnet, ibnodes, iblinkinfo
- Firmware on switches, BlueField, NICs, and optics
- Storage benchmarking: throughput, IOPS, latency, checkpoint performance
Hands-on:
- Take NVIDIA Cable Validation Tool Fundamentals.
- Take Data Center Management Made Easy with NVIDIA UFM.
- Run local/Lambda disk testing with fio.
- Create an AI-cluster cable matrix: server NIC → leaf switch → spine switch.
- Build a UFM dashboard requirements document.
Example storage lab:
fio --name=write-test \
  --filename=testfile \
  --size=4G \
  --rw=write \
  --bs=1M \
  --direct=1 \
  --iodepth=16
Deliverable: fabric-cable-storage-validation-runbook.md
NVIDIA’s AI Networking learning path currently includes CVT, UFM, InfiniBand, RDMA, and BlueField material. NVIDIA AI Networking learning path
Week 8 — Capstone: AI cluster acceptance and incident simulation
Exam focus: Full blueprint integration
Create a complete customer scenario:
A financial-services customer is deploying an 8-node, 64-GPU HGX cluster for regulated GenAI inference and model training.

Build these artifacts:
1. AI-factory reference architecture diagram
2. Day-0 physical deployment checklist
3. Day-1 provisioning and BCM/Slurm/Kubernetes design
4. Firmware/driver compatibility matrix
5. GPU/NVLink/NCCL/HPL/storage validation plan
6. DCGM/UFM monitoring design
7. Troubleshooting matrix
8. Customer handoff/production-readiness report
Practice five incident investigations:
- GPU missing from nvidia-smi
- GPU pod/job cannot obtain GPU resources
- NCCL bandwidth below baseline
- InfiniBand link or transceiver fault
- Storage throughput slows model checkpointing
Deliverable: ncp-aii-capstone-ai-cluster-acceptance-report.md
Certification readiness check
Take the NCP-AII exam only when you can answer these without notes:
- What happens from rack installation to a validated production cluster?
- How do you validate GPU, PCIe, NVLink/NVSwitch, NIC, fabric, and storage?
- When would you use BCM, Slurm, Kubernetes, and Run:ai?
- How do you distinguish a GPU, firmware, power, cable, network, or storage problem?
- What does each tool validate: nvidia-smi, DCGM, NCCL, HPL, ClusterKit, UFM, CVT?
- How would you explain an AI-factory design to a customer?
Your certification priority remains NCP-AII first. It gives you the broadest match to the NVIDIA Solutions Architect positions. NCP-AIN is the logical second certification after you build deeper UFM, InfiniBand, RoCE, Spectrum-X, and BlueField experience.
